  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The models are calibrated using stated preference survey data rather than observed behavior during an actual evacuation event.",
      "what": "This study models evacuation mode choices for a hypothetical volcanic eruption in Auckland, New Zealand, using stated preference survey data. It compares logistic regression against four machine learning algorithms—Random Forest, Support Vector Machine, Probabilistic Neural Network, and Classification and Regression Tree—finding that Random Forest achieves the highest predictive accuracy. The analysis identifies vehicle ownership, evacuation timing, and route or destination preferences as the primary determinants of how residents choose to evacuate.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that machine learning classifiers, particularly Random Forest, provide superior predictive performance over traditional logistic regression for volcanic evacuation planning, allowing emergency managers to more accurately anticipate transport demand and resource allocation."
    }
  },
